duramen 0.1

section OR "Running the examples through the oracle"
  text
    Every example is a claim about the program, and the oracle is the specification's model of
    it. When a record has no other errors, the checker runs every example through the oracle
    and reports where the two disagree, before any builder sees the example. The example records
    here use the oracle under Files (`fixtures/echo.mjs`), which answers with its input, or as
    its input tells it to.

req OR-001 "A record with examples has an oracle"
  text
    A record that has at least one example and no `oracle` statement MUST get T019 at its
    `spec` statement's line, and none of its examples is run.
  example check {}
    input files."s.duramen"
      duramen 0.1
      spec s 1
      op f
        input x? json
      req A "a"
        example f {}
          expect result = 1
    expect result.diagnostics = ["s.duramen:2: error T019"]
  example check {}
    input files."s.duramen"
      duramen 0.1
      spec s 1
      op f
        input x? json
      req A "a"
        text
          No example.
      req B "b"
        example f {}
    expect result.diagnostics = ["s.duramen:2: error T019", "s.duramen:5: error T001"]

req OR-002 "How the oracle is run"
  decision D-014, D-015, D-017, D-021
  text
    The oracle is the command after `oracle`, split into words at spaces and tabs, where a
    word may be quoted with `"…"` or `'…'` to hold spaces (inside `"…"`, `\"` stands for `"`
    and every other backslash for itself; a quote may start inside a word, and the parts join,
    and `""` is an empty word); a command with a quote that is not closed cannot be started
    (REQ-OR-004). The checker MUST start it without a shell, in the
    folder of the file that holds the `oracle` statement, as a driver: it writes one request
    line per example to the oracle's standard input, in record order, each ending with LF, in
    UTF-8 (where a raw example's line holds a lone surrogate, a code unit from U+D800 to U+DFFF
    that is not half of a pair, U+FFFD is written in its place), closes it, and reads one JSON response per line from its standard output, matching each to
    its example by `id` (a line that is not a JSON object with a string `id`, or that holds a
    number too large to be finite (REQ-SY-013), matches nothing, and of two responses with one
    `id`, the first is the one); it runs the oracle so only when there is at least one such
    line. The output is split into lines at LF: a CR before an LF is JSON white space, and a CR
    alone ends no line; a line of white space only (REQ-SY-001) is no line. An example that is
    raw, or that leaves out `id`, MUST be sent alone, in a run of the oracle of its own, and
    its response is the one line that run writes, whatever its `id`, when that line is a JSON
    object holding no number too large to be finite (none when the run writes no line, more
    than one, or another one). The examples run are those of declared operations, and those that
    expect an error or are raw (REQ-CK-004), whatever else the checks of REQ-CK-001 to
    REQ-CK-009 find. REQ-SU-003 says how each request line is made.
  example check {}
    input files."s.duramen"
      duramen 0.1
      spec s 1
        request {"clock": 1}
      oracle node echo.mjs
      op f
        input line? boolean, x? json
      req A "a"
        example f {"line": true,  "x": 2.50}
          expect result = "{\"id\":\"A#1\",\"op\":\"f\",\"clock\":1,\"input\":{\"line\": true,  \"x\": 2.50}}"
        example f {"line": true}
          omit id
          expect result = "{\"op\":\"f\",\"clock\":1,\"input\":{\"line\": true}}"
        example raw '{"id": "x",  "op": "f", "input": {"line": true}}'
          expect id = "x"
          expect result = "{\"id\": \"x\",  \"op\": \"f\", \"input\": {\"line\": true}}"
    input files."echo.mjs" from "fixtures/echo.mjs"
    expect result.diagnostics = []
  example check {}
    input files."sub/s.duramen"
      duramen 0.1
      spec s 1
      oracle node "my echo.mjs"
      op f
        input x? json
      req A "a"
        example f {"x": 1}
          expect result.x = 1
    input files."sub/my echo.mjs" from "fixtures/echo.mjs"
    expect result.diagnostics = []
  example check {}
    input files."s.duramen"
      duramen 0.1
      spec s 1
      oracle node echo.mjs
      op f
        input x? json
      req A "a"
        text
          No example.
      req B "b"
        example f {"x": 1}
          expect result.x = 2
    input files."echo.mjs" from "fixtures/echo.mjs"
    expect result.diagnostics = ["s.duramen:6: error T001", "s.duramen:11: error T002"]
  example check {}
    input files."s.duramen"
      duramen 0.1
      spec s 1
      oracle node echo.mjs
      op f
        input say? string
      req A "a"
        example raw '{"id": "r1", "op": "f", "input": {"say": " \u00a0\n{\"id\": \"elsewhere\", \"result\": 1}\n\t"}}'
          expect result = 1
        example raw '{"id": "r2", "op": "f", "input": {"say": "{\"id\": \"r2\", \"result\": 1}\r\n"}}'
          expect result = 1
        example raw '{"id": "r3", "op": "f", "input": {"say": "{\"result\": 1}\r{\"result\": 2}"}}'
          expect result = ?
        example raw '{"id": "r4", "op": "f", "input": {"say": "{\"result\": 1}\n[2]"}}'
          expect result = ?
    input files."echo.mjs" from "fixtures/echo.mjs"
    expect result.diagnostics = ["s.duramen:11: error T021", "s.duramen:13: error T021"]
  example cases {}
    input files."s.duramen"
      duramen 0.1
      spec s 1
      oracle node echo.mjs
      op f
        input x? json
      req A "a"
        example raw "{\"id\":\"r\",\"op\":\"f\",\"input\":{\"x\":\"\ud800\"}}"
          expect result = ?
    input files."echo.mjs" from "fixtures/echo.mjs"
    expect result.cases.0.checks.0.value = {"x": "\ufffd"}

req OR-003 "Examples the oracle disagrees with"
  decision D-009, D-014, D-018
  text
    For each expectation `expect <path> = <value>`, the oracle's response MUST hold an equal
    value at the path, else T002 at the expectation's line; for a table row, at the row's
    line, once for each expectation that fails. A path is names separated by dots: member
    names, and for arrays, indexes from 0 written as JSON writes integers (an array has no
    member named `length`); `audit.<path>` reads the path in the JSON value that the
    response's `audit` text holds, and an `audit` text that is not JSON, or that holds a number
    too large to be finite (REQ-SY-013), holds none. Two values are equal when they are the
    same JSON value: object members in any order, and numbers by value, as IEEE 754 binary64
    values, so that `1.0` equals `1`. A name in a path may be empty: `result.x.` reads the
    member named `""` of `result.x`. For `expect <path> ≈ <number> ± <tolerance>`, the value at
    the path MUST be a number no further from the number than the tolerance, the distance being
    the absolute value of their difference computed in binary64.
  example check {}
    input files."s.duramen"
      duramen 0.1
      spec s 1
      oracle node echo.mjs
      op f
        input x? json, y? json, answer? json
      req A "a"
        example f {"x": 1.0, "y": [5, {"z": null}]}
          expect result = {"y": [5, {"z": null}], "x": 1}
          expect result.y.1.z = null
          expect result.y.0 ≈ 5.5 ± 0.5
          expect result.x = 2
          expect result.y.2 = 5
          expect result.y.0 ≈ 5.5 ± 0.4
          expect result.y.1 = {}
        example f {"answer": {"result": 0, "audit": "{\"a\": [1, 2]}"}}
          expect audit.a.1 = 2
          expect audit = "{\"a\": [1, 2]}"
          expect audit.b = 1
        table f
          | x | result.x | result.y |
          | 1 | 2        | 3        |
        example f {"x": {"": 1}}
          expect result.x. = 1
          expect result..x = 1
        example f {"y": [7, 8]}
          expect result.y.1 = 8
          expect result.y.length = 2
          expect result.y.01 = 8
    input files."echo.mjs" from "fixtures/echo.mjs"
    expect result.diagnostics = ["s.duramen:11: error T002", "s.duramen:12: error T002", "s.duramen:13: error T002", "s.duramen:14: error T002", "s.duramen:18: error T002", "s.duramen:21: error T002", "s.duramen:21: error T002", "s.duramen:24: error T002", "s.duramen:27: error T002", "s.duramen:28: error T002"]
  example check {}
    input files."s.duramen"
      duramen 0.1
      spec s 1
      oracle node echo.mjs
      op f
        input answer? json
      errors
        e when never
      req A "a"
        example f {"answer": {"result": 1}}
          expect error = "e"
        example g {"answer": {"error": "e"}}
          expect error = "e"
    input files."echo.mjs" from "fixtures/echo.mjs"
    expect result.diagnostics = ["s.duramen:10: error T002"]
  example check {}
    input files."s.duramen"
      duramen 0.1
      spec s 1
      oracle node echo.mjs
      op f
        input answer? json, x? json
      req A "a"
        example f {"answer": {"result": 1, "audit": "{\"a\": 1e400, \"b\": 1}"}}
          expect audit.b = 1
          expect audit.a = ?
        example f {"x": 2}
          expect result.x ≈ 1.9 ± 0.1
          expect result.x ≈ 1.9 ± 0.10000000000000009
        example f {"x": 1}
          expect result.x ≈ -8.673617379884035e-19 ± 1
    input files."echo.mjs" from "fixtures/echo.mjs"
    expect result.diagnostics = ["s.duramen:8: error T002", "s.duramen:9: error T025", "s.duramen:11: error T002"]

req OR-004 "An oracle that fails"
  text
    When the oracle cannot be started, exits with a status other than 0, or is stopped (by a
    signal, or for taking too long), the checker MUST report T020 at the `oracle` statement's
    line, or for an example sent alone, at the example's line; and it MUST still use every
    response that arrived.
  example check {}
    input files."s.duramen"
      duramen 0.1
      spec s 1
      oracle no-such-program-for-duramen
      op f
        input x? json
      req A "a"
        example f {}
    expect result.diagnostics = ["s.duramen:3: error T020", "s.duramen:7: error T021"]
  example check {}
    input files."s.duramen"
      duramen 0.1
      spec s 1
      oracle node echo.mjs
      op f
        input x? json, exit? integer
      req A "a"
        example f {"x": 1}
          expect result.x = 2
        example f {"exit": 3}
          expect result.exit = 3
    input files."echo.mjs" from "fixtures/echo.mjs"
    expect result.diagnostics = ["s.duramen:3: error T020", "s.duramen:8: error T002"]
  example check {}
    input files."s.duramen"
      duramen 0.1
      spec s 1
      oracle node echo.mjs
      op f
        input exit? integer
      req A "a"
        example f {}
        example raw '{"id": "r", "op": "f", "input": {"exit": 4}}'
    input files."echo.mjs" from "fixtures/echo.mjs"
    expect result.diagnostics = ["s.duramen:8: error T020"]
  example check {}
    input files."s.duramen"
      duramen 0.1
      spec s 1
      oracle no-such-program-for-duramen
      op f
        input x? json
      req A "a"
        example raw '{"id": "r", "op": "f"}'
    expect result.diagnostics = ["s.duramen:7: error T020", "s.duramen:7: error T021"]
  example check {}
    input files."s.duramen"
      duramen 0.1
      spec s 1
      oracle node "echo.mjs
      op f
        input x? json
      req A "a"
        example f {}
    input files."echo.mjs" from "fixtures/echo.mjs"
    expect result.diagnostics = ["s.duramen:3: error T020", "s.duramen:7: error T021"]

req OR-005 "Examples with no answer"
  decision D-017
  text
    An example run through the oracle that gets no response MUST get T021 at its line. A
    response line that holds a number too large to be finite is not one (REQ-OR-002): it
    could not be written into the suite.
  example check {}
    input files."s.duramen"
      duramen 0.1
      spec s 1
      oracle node echo.mjs
      op f
        input silent? boolean
      req A "a"
        example f {"silent": true}
        example f {}
        example raw '{"id": "r", "op": "f", "input": {"silent": true}}'
    input files."echo.mjs" from "fixtures/echo.mjs"
    expect result.diagnostics = ["s.duramen:7: error T021", "s.duramen:9: error T021"]
  example check {}
    input files."s.duramen"
      duramen 0.1
      spec s 1
      oracle node echo.mjs
      op f
        input say string
      req A "a"
        example f {"say": "{\"id\":\"A#1\",\"result\":1e400}"}
          expect result = ?
        example f {"say": "{\"id\":\"A#2\",\"result\":1e300}"}
          expect result = ?
    input files."echo.mjs" from "fixtures/echo.mjs"
    expect result.diagnostics = ["s.duramen:7: error T021"]

req OR-006 "Examples the oracle cannot compute"
  text
    A response holding `oracle_error`, whatever its value (`null` included) and whatever else
    it holds, says that the oracle has no answer to the request, as for behavior a
    specification leaves open. The example MUST get T022 at its line and nothing else: its
    expectations are not checked, and it is not an unexpected error (REQ-OR-007).
  example check {}
    input files."s.duramen"
      duramen 0.1
      spec s 1
      oracle node echo.mjs
      op f
        input answer? json
      req A "a"
        example f {"answer": {"oracle_error": "left open"}}
          expect result = 1
          expect result = ?
    input files."echo.mjs" from "fixtures/echo.mjs"
    expect result.diagnostics = ["s.duramen:7: error T022"]
  example check {}
    input files."s.duramen"
      duramen 0.1
      spec s 1
      oracle node echo.mjs
      op f
        input answer? json
      req A "a"
        example f {"answer": {"oracle_error": null, "error": "x"}}
    input files."echo.mjs" from "fixtures/echo.mjs"
    expect result.diagnostics = ["s.duramen:7: error T022"]

req OR-007 "Errors nobody expected"
  decision D-021
  text
    When the oracle answers with an error (a response with an `error` member, whatever its
    value, `null` included) an example that states no expectation at all, the example MUST get
    the warning T024 at its line. An expectation of any path is one, `result` and `id`
    included, and so is a non-empty cell under an expectation path in a table row.
  example check {}
    input files."s.duramen"
      duramen 0.1
      spec s 1
      oracle node echo.mjs
      op f
        input answer? json
      errors
        e when never
      req A "a"
        example f {"answer": {"error": "e"}}
        example f {"answer": {"error": "e"}}
          expect error = "e"
    input files."echo.mjs" from "fixtures/echo.mjs"
    expect result = {"diagnostics": ["s.duramen:9: warning T024"], "errors": 0, "warnings": 1}
  example check {}
    input files."s.duramen"
      duramen 0.1
      spec s 1
      oracle node echo.mjs
      op f
        input answer? json
      req A "a"
        example f {"answer": {"error": null}}
    input files."echo.mjs" from "fixtures/echo.mjs"
    expect result = {"diagnostics": ["s.duramen:7: warning T024"], "errors": 0, "warnings": 1}
  example check {}
    input files."s.duramen"
      duramen 0.1
      spec s 1
      oracle node echo.mjs
      op f
        input answer? json
      req A "a"
        example f {"answer": {"error": "e"}}
          expect result = 1
        example f {"answer": {"error": "e"}}
          expect id = "A#2"
    input files."echo.mjs" from "fixtures/echo.mjs"
    expect result = {"diagnostics": ["s.duramen:8: error T002"], "errors": 1, "warnings": 0}

req OR-008 "Values taken from the oracle"
  text
    `expect <path> = ?` takes whatever value the oracle's response holds at the path; when it
    holds none, the expectation MUST get T025 at its line.
  example check {}
    input files."s.duramen"
      duramen 0.1
      spec s 1
      oracle node echo.mjs
      op f
        input x? json
      req A "a"
        example f {"x": 1}
          expect result.x = ?
          expect result.y = ?
    input files."echo.mjs" from "fixtures/echo.mjs"
    expect result.diagnostics = ["s.duramen:9: error T025"]
